messages:
alex: quick question — we're seeing inconsistent abort semantics across SDKs when streaming + retries. Node uses AbortController, Go uses context.Context, but behavior differs when a stream is aborted while a background retry is queued. Example (Node):
```js
const ac = new AbortController();
const res = await client.stream({ prompt }, { signal: ac.signal });
// then ac.abort() while response streaming
```
In Node the stream/socket closes quickly, but the SDK sometimes triggers a retry which launches another request (and we end up double-sending heavy work). :thinking: @maya any guidance?

maya: thanks — yeah this was an open area. Short answer: abort should cancel the active request and not trigger retries. The subtlety: our retry layer runs outside the streaming socket lifecycle in some implementations. Need to ensure abort signal flows into retry decision.

chris: +1. On the runtime side we also see cases where the client cancels but the worker queue still executes the task because cancellation didn't propagate across our RPC boundary. Sample log from runtime:
```
[2026-02-14T18:02:12Z] req=0xabc started -> queued on worker 23
[2026-02-14T18:02:13Z] req=0xabc client-cancelled
[2026-02-14T18:02:14Z] req=0xabc worker started (no cancel token) -> expensive compute
```
We should short-circuit queued tasks if the originating request was aborted.

alex: thanks — so two things: (1) client SDKs must not treat abort as transient network error that qualifies for retry, (2) server/routing layer must accept an abort/timeout signal and cancel queued work if possible. For SDKs, should we standardize: abort => immediate no-retry error with code "aborted"? That would make behavior consistent across languages.

maya: I like standardizing an error code. Proposal: when AbortController/context cancelled, SDK returns an error with .code = "ABORTED" and does not perform retries. For streaming, emit a terminal chunk with meta { cancelled: true } then close. Pseudocode (Node):
```js
fetchWithAbort(url, { signal }) {
  try {
    return await fetch(url, { signal })
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === 'AbortError') throw createSdkError('ABORTED', 'request aborted by client')
    throw err
  }
}
```
This also requires tests to assert no additional requests made after abort.

chris: sounds good. One more corner: what if a retry policy is "always-retry-idempotent" and request body is idempotent but user aborted? I agree aborted should override retry policy. Also need telemetry event so we can track cancelled vs failed.

alex: agreed. Action items: @maya can you open an SDK RFC/PR that: (a) defines ABORTED code, (b) wiring for abort -> no-retry across node/py/go, (c) tests for streaming cancellation and retry suppression. @chris can you draft runtime TODO to propagate client cancel across RPC and short-circuit queued workers. I'll add tickets.

maya: will do, opening PR today and pinging reviewers. cc @chris for runtime changes. :rocket:

lm-ci-bot: CI started for maya/abort-standardization -> build #1284 :white_check_mark:

alex: thanks — if folks can thumbs-up the PR when it's up that'd help unblock the runtime work. :thumbsup:
